/-
# Fixed points of cyclic automorphisms of free Burnside groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 17.70)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 17.70 (V. D. Mazurov): let `α` be an automorphism of prime order `q` of
an infinite free Burnside group `G = B(q, p)` of prime exponent `p` such that `α` cyclically
permutes the free generators of `G`. Is it true that `α` fixes some non-trivial element of `G`?
(Editors' comment: this is proved for `q = 2`.)
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.Basic
public import Mathlib.Basic.Finite.Defs
public import Mathlib.Data.Nat.Prime.Defs
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.FreeGroup.Basic
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.QuotientGroup.Defs
public import Mathlib.Logic.Equiv.Fin.Rotate

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FreeBurnsideCyclicAutomorphismFixedPointKourovka17Problem70Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 17.70 (V. D. Mazurov). UnsolvedMath record
UM-00701372 (`KOU-17.70`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

`B(m, n)` is `Fₘ` modulo the normal subgroup generated by all `n`-th powers. The automorphism is the
one induced by `xᵢ ↦ xᵢ₊₁ (mod q)` (`finRotate q`); any other cyclic permutation of the `q`
generators is conjugate to it by a permutation of the generators, which induces an automorphism of
`B(q, p)` carrying fixed points to fixed points. Fixed points are written through representatives `w
∈ F_q` of elements of `B(q, p)`.
-/

/-- The free Burnside group `B(m, n) = Fₘ / Fₘⁿ`: the free group of rank `m` modulo the normal
subgroup generated by all `n`-th powers. -/
abbrev FreeBurnsideGroup (m n : ℕ) : Type :=
  FreeGroup (Fin m) ⧸ Subgroup.normalClosure (Set.range fun w : FreeGroup (Fin m) => w ^ n)

/-- Kourovka 17.70 (V. D. Mazurov): for primes `q, p` with `B(q, p)` infinite, the automorphism of
`B(q, p)` induced by the cyclic permutation `xᵢ ↦ xᵢ₊₁ (mod q)` of the free generators fixes some
nontrivial element; the fixed points are written through representatives `w ∈ F_q`. -/
def cyclicAutomorphismFixesNontrivial : Prop :=
  ∀ q p : ℕ, q.Prime → p.Prime → Infinite (FreeBurnsideGroup q p) →
    ∃ w : FreeGroup (Fin q), (w : FreeBurnsideGroup q p) ≠ 1 ∧
      ((FreeGroup.map (finRotate q) w : FreeGroup (Fin q)) : FreeBurnsideGroup q p) = w

/--
Resolved false: Atabekyan (Theorem 1.1) proves that for every odd `m ≥ 3` (in particular every prime
`m ≥ 3`) there is `q_m` such that for every prime `q > q_m` the automorphism of the free Burnside
group `B(m, q)` cyclically permuting the `m` free generators has no nontrivial fixed points, and
these groups are infinite by Adian's theorem (`B(m, n)` is infinite for odd `n ≥ 665`, `m > 1`). In
the notation of the problem the answer is negative for every prime `q ≥ 3` and all large primes `p`;
the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) answers 17.70 "No, it does not for all `q ≥ 3` and all
sufficiently large `p`". Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group
Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 17.70 (V. D. Mazurov);
resolution: V. S. Atabekyan, Mazurov's question on fixed points of automorphisms of free Burnside
groups, preprint, arXiv:2609.33941 (2026), Theorem 1.1 (for every odd m ≥ 3 there is q_m such that
for every prime q > q_m the automorphism of B(m, q) of order m cyclically permuting the free
generators has no nontrivial fixed points; B(m, n) is infinite for all odd n ≥ 665 and m > 1 by
Adian's theorem, as recalled there); the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) marks 17.70 solved (No, it
does not for all q ≥ 3 and all sufficiently large p), https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33941.
-/
public theorem_wanted cyclicAutomorphismFixesNontrivial_refuted :
    ¬ cyclicAutomorphismFixesNontrivial

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FreeBurnsideCyclicAutomorphismFixedPointKourovka17Problem70Wanted
